.TH FAIL2ZIG.TOML 5 "2026-10-21" "fail2zig 0.4.8" "File Formats"
.
.SH NAME
fail2zig.toml \- fail2zig daemon configuration file
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/fail2zig/config.toml
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fail2zig.toml
is the configuration file for the
.BR fail2zig (1)
intrusion prevention daemon. The file uses a strict subset of the TOML format.
.PP
Unknown keys are rejected with line and column context. Unknown sections are
rejected. Values are strictly typed, with integer seconds or quoted strings
accepted for the duration keys listed under DURATION SYNTAX.
.PP
The file is divided into three section types:
.IP \(bu 2
.B [global]
\(em daemon-wide settings (log level, socket path, memory ceiling, metrics listener, firewall backend).
.IP \(bu 2
.B [defaults]
\(em default values applied to every jail unless overridden.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR [jails. \fIname\fR ]
\(em one section per jail, overriding any defaults as needed.
.PP
Validate the configuration without starting the daemon:
.PP
.nf
sudo fail2zig --validate-config --config /etc/fail2zig/config.toml
.fi
.
.SH GLOBAL SECTION
.TP
.B log_level
String. One of
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.BR warn ,
.BR err .
Default:
.IR info .
.TP
.B log_format
String.
.B text
or
.BR json .
Default:
.IR text .
With
.BR json ,
every daemon log line is one JSON object on a line of its own with the fields
.B ts
(RFC 3339, UTC, milliseconds),
.BR level ,
.B scope
and
.BR msg ,
plus the keys of an audit line as fields. A line cut at the length limit is still a
valid object and carries
.BR \(dqtruncated\(dq:true .
Any other value is refused with the hint
.BR "expected text or json" .
A change is restart-only:
.B fail2zig reload
reports
.BR restart_required .
.TP
.B pid_file
String (absolute path). Deprecated compatibility key; an explicit setting logs
a warning and no PID file is created. This setting can be omitted; no replacement
setting is needed. With systemd, query the daemon's PID using:
.nf
systemctl show fail2zig.service --property=MainPID --value
.fi
.TP
.B socket_path
String (absolute path). Unix domain socket for
.BR fail2zig (1)
administration commands.
Default:
.IR /run/fail2zig/fail2zig.sock .
Mode 0660; group
.B fail2zig
if that group exists.
.TP
.B websocket_max_clients
Integer (1–1024). Maximum simultaneous WebSocket clients. Default: 16.
.TP
.B state_file
String (absolute path). Durable SQLite database containing source positions,
records, decisions, owners, effects and history. It is created or opened at
startup with mode 0600. Corrupt or unsupported state is refused rather than
silently reset. The file cannot grow past 1 GiB (262,144 pages of 4 KiB); a
state file already past that size is refused at startup. When a commit fails
because the file is full, the daemon pauses ingestion, retries after a delay that
doubles up to 30 seconds and returns to healthy once recovery completes. Rules
already in the kernel stay in place meanwhile, and
.B fail2zig status
shows the storage phase. The daemon runs no VACUUM, so the file does not shrink;
rows removed by retention (see
.BR history_retention ,
in seconds, default 86400) free pages for reuse inside it. Watch the file size.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/fail2zig/state.bin .
.IP
With the shipped systemd unit, changing this path also requires a matching
service drop-in: keep
.BR ProtectSystem=strict ,
reset
.B ReadWritePaths=
then grant only the dedicated custom parent and
.IR /run/fail2zig ,
and clear
.B StateDirectory=
to stop systemd creating the unused default directory. The custom parent must
already exist, belong to the service UID and not be group/world writable;
database files must be private and owned by that UID. Shell writability does
not establish access inside the service. Stop all writers and preserve the
SQLite database and existing WAL/SHM siblings together before relocation.
Automatic installer upgrades refuse custom paths. See the complete procedure:
.UR https://github.com/ul0gic/fail2zig/blob/main/docs/operations/installation.md#custom-state-directory-under-systemd
Custom state directory under systemd
.UE .
.TP
.B history_retention
Integer (seconds, 0 or more). How long application history is kept. Older
history is removed while storage is healthy. Default: 86400.
.TP
.B history_max_matches
Integer (0\(en1024). Total retained matches per address across all jails.
Default: 10. A jail override can narrow its share but cannot raise this total.
An accepted
.B fail2zig reload
applies changes without resetting retry state or source positions.
Zero hides matched lines immediately; bounded cleanup removes stored detail
after existing history-consumer dependencies permit it. Decisions and confirmed
event identities remain available. Raising the limit cannot recover deleted lines.
.TP
.B memory_ceiling_mb
Integer. Total configured memory ceiling used to admit bounded native source,
detection, retry and administration plans. Startup refuses configurations whose
required bounded plan does not fit. Durable receipts, enforcement intent and
active ownership are not evicted to meet the ceiling. Minimum: 16. Default: 64.
.TP
.B native_memory_ceiling_mb
Integer. Memory ceiling, in MiB, that startup admits the native plan against: sources,
retry state, the 65,536-ban live capacity, effect ownership and firewall readback.
The plan for 65,536 live bans is charged about 337 MiB with two enforcing jails and
about 438 MiB with sixteen, so a ceiling below the plan makes startup refuse with
.BR NativeMemoryAdmission .
Minimum: 1. Default: 512.
.TP
.B native_fd_ceiling
Integer. Most file descriptors the native plan may use. At start the daemon raises
its soft
.B RLIMIT_NOFILE
to this value, never past the hard limit, then admits the plan against the lower of
the two; each file jail is charged for eight sources, so a plan that does not fit
makes startup refuse with
.BR NativeFdAdmission .
Minimum: 1. Default: 8192, enough for 64 file jails.
.TP
.B metrics_enabled
Boolean. When
.IR false ,
no HTTP or WebSocket listener is bound; the daemon logs
.I "http: disabled by config"
and the startup banner shows
.IR http=off .
IPC,
.BR fail2zig (1)
administration commands,
jails, bans, and state are unaffected. Default:
.IR true .
.TP
.B metrics_bind
String (IP address). Address the HTTP metrics server binds to. Default:
.IR 127.0.0.1 .
Do not expose publicly without additional access controls.
.TP
.B metrics_port
Integer (1\(en65535). Port for the HTTP server. Default:
.IR 9100 .
The server exposes
.IR /metrics ,
.IR /api/status ,
and
.IR /events
(WebSocket). 0 is rejected; it does not disable the endpoint. Use
.BR "metrics_enabled = false" .
.TP
.B firewall
String. Which firewall backend to use.
.I auto
probes nftables, then ipset, then iptables and uses the first that is usable
when no firewall installation is recorded in the state database.
With an existing installation,
.I auto
uses its recorded backend and does not switch to another backend if it becomes
unavailable. This binding survives daemon restarts and host reboots, including
when there are no active bans.
.IR nftables ,
.IR ipset ,
or
.I iptables
probes only that backend; if it is unusable,
.B on_no_backend
decides. There is no fallback to another backend. Unknown values are
rejected with line and column context. Default:
.IR auto .
.PP
An explicit backend that conflicts with the recorded installation is rejected
before backend probing. Restore the recorded backend or
.I auto
with the existing state database. Changing this setting does not migrate
firewall ownership; do not delete the database to switch backends.
The existing
.B on_no_backend
policy controls whether admission failure exits or runs degraded as log-only.
.TP
.B firewall_namespace
Absolute path selecting the network namespace whose firewall state is owned by
this daemon. The built-in default is the daemon's current network namespace.
The daemon verifies that the selected namespace is still its current namespace
and never enters another namespace. Explicit
.I /proc/self
and
.I /proc/thread-self
aliases are rejected because they are not stable external anchors. Custom
selectors and service overrides that move the daemon between namespaces are
unsupported.
An existing installation also records the selector spelling. A changed selector
is rejected even if it currently names the same namespace; restore the previous
setting with the existing state database.
.TP
.B on_no_backend
String. What happens when an enforcing jail exists but no usable backend was
found.
.I fail-closed
exits with the cause logged.
.I log-only
keeps running DEGRADED: bans are logged, not applied, and
.BR fail2zig (1)
.B status
shows
.IR "Protection: DEGRADED (cause)" .
Default:
.IR fail-closed .
.
.SH DEFAULTS SECTION
All keys in this section apply to every jail unless overridden in the
.BI [jails. name ]
section.
.TP
.B bantime
Integer seconds or a quoted duration. Ban duration for a first offense.
Minimum finite value: 1. Default: 600. The string "permanent" selects an
explicit permanent ban; no other duration key accepts it.
.TP
.B findtime
Integer seconds or a quoted duration. Sliding window for counting failures.
Minimum: 1. Default: 600.
.TP
.B maxretry
Integer. Number of failures within
.B findtime
required to trigger a ban. Minimum: 1. Default: 5.
The daemon holds at most 65,536 live bans across all enabled jails, in one shared pool,
and tracks up to 65,536 further subjects that have not yet reached
.BR maxretry ,
including subjects whose ban has expired and that retirement has not yet removed.
A subject that is banned stops counting against tracking capacity. Together the two
pools never exceed 131,072 stored subjects.
.IP
When the tracking pool is full and a new subject must be tracked, the daemon evicts the
idle tracked subject with the oldest last activity in the same commit and tracks the new
one. An idle subject has no live ban; a subject whose ban has
expired and that retirement has not yet removed counts as idle. A subject with a live ban
is never evicted. Each eviction adds one to the jail's
.B fail2zig_jail_pool_evictions_total
counter, and the daemon logs at most one warning per jail every 60 seconds:
.BI "native: jail=" name " tracking pool full: evicted " n " idle subjects in the last 60 s" .
When no idle subject can be evicted, or when 65,536 bans are live and a failure would ban
another subject, the failure is refused as retryable backpressure
.RB ( RetryCapacity )
until a ban expires or a subject is retired.
.B fail2zig status
shows the occupancy of both pools against their limits.
.TP
.B banaction
Deprecated compatibility input. Use
.B enforce
to state whether the jail is configured to enforce or observe.
.BR nftables ,
.BR iptables ,
and
.B ipset
all mean enforce with the backend selected by
.BR global.firewall ;
the names are accepted for fail2ban compatibility and do not select a backend.
.B log-only
observes and never touches the firewall. Default:
.IR nftables .
An explicit setting emits a deprecation warning. It remains supported for existing
configurations; new configurations should use
.BR enforce .
.TP
.B enforce
Boolean. Configured enforcement intent, inherited from
.B defaults.enforce
unless overridden in a jail. True selects enforcement using
.BR global.firewall ;
false configures log-only observation. The effective configured intent is reported
separately from observed protection: a jail may be configured to enforce while
protection is unconfirmed because backend or daemon criteria are not met. Conversely,
.B active
protection reflects existing daemon criteria and is not an independent per-jail
kernel verification. Default: true.
.TP
.B ignoreip
String array. CIDR ranges and exact IPs that are never banned. Example:
.IR "[\(dq127.0.0.1/8\(dq, \(dq::1\(dq, \(dq10.0.0.0/8\(dq]" .
Default: empty. An entry is an address or CIDR, permanent while listed, or an address or
CIDR followed by whitespace and a duration, for example
.IR "\(dq203.0.113.9 1d\(dq" ,
which excludes the address for that long under the rule in TIMED ENTRIES. An entry
without a duration takes
.B ignoretime
when it is set. An expired entry stays in the file and excludes nothing, and
.B fail2zig ignore list
shows it as
.BR expired .
A host name entry never carries a duration. An entry whose duration is not valid is
refused with
.BI "jails." name ".ignoreip: invalid duration in '" text '
(the whole entry; a
.B [defaults]
entry has no jail prefix). On a jail with
.BR rule_files ,
an entry with a duration is refused with
.BI "jails." name ".ignoreip: '" text "': a duration is not supported with rule_files" .
An edit applies from the next event after
.B fail2zig reload
or a restart, and never alters an existing ban. An address may not be in both
.B ignoreip
and
.B banip
of one jail; see
.BR banip .
.TP
.B banip
String array. Addresses and CIDR ranges the jail bans while they are listed. An entry is
.I \(dqaddress-or-CIDR\(dq
(permanent while listed) or
.I \(dqaddress-or-CIDR duration\(dq
(timed, for example
.IR "\(dq198.51.100.0/24 1d\(dq" ;
the duration uses the syntax of
.BR bantime ,
is finite and at least 1s, and runs under the rule in TIMED ENTRIES). Host names are not
accepted. A jail list replaces the
.B [defaults]
list, as for
.BR ignoreip .
Default: empty.
.IP
At start and at every
.B fail2zig reload
each entry is a live ban with origin
.B config
in that jail, permanent for a permanent entry and ending at the stored deadline for a timed
one. An entry takes over an existing ban of the same address in the jail, whatever its
origin, and replaces its expiry with its own, even when the ban it replaces would have lasted
longer. Removing an entry and reloading lifts its ban at once; a later detection bans the
address normally. When a timed entry runs out, its ban is lifted, the entry stays in the
file and does nothing, and
.B fail2zig list
shows it as
.BR expired .
While a config ban is live, a detection of the address is receipted and changes nothing,
and
.B fail2zig ban
or
.B fail2zig unban
of it is refused with
.BR "banned by config: edit banip" .
A reload that changes only
.B banip
is applied live.
.IP
A jail holds at most 1,024 effective entries; more is refused with
.BI "jails." name ".banip: more than 1024 entries" .
An entry that is not an address or CIDR is refused with
.BI "jails." name ".banip: invalid entry '" text ' ,
and one whose duration is not valid with
.BI "jails." name ".banip: invalid duration in '" text ' .
An address in both the effective
.B ignoreip
and the effective
.B banip
of one jail, as equal entries or one containing the other and with or without a duration,
is a configuration error: the daemon refuses to start, and
.B fail2zig reload
reports
.B rejected
with a reason naming the jail and the address.
.TP
.B ignoreself
Boolean. Adds every IPv4 and IPv6 address of the host's interfaces, loopback included, to
the jail's effective ignore list, where
.B fail2zig ignore list
shows them with source
.BR self .
The daemon reads the addresses from the kernel at start and at every
.BR "fail2zig reload" ;
an address assigned after that counts as
.B self
from the next reload. If the kernel cannot be read, the daemon logs a warning and the jail
has no
.B self
entries until the next reload. These entries do not count against the 128-entry ignore
limit. A change is applied live by
.BR "fail2zig reload" .
Default:
.IR true .
.TP
.B ignoretime
Integer seconds or a quoted duration. The default life of an exclusion that carries no
duration of its own: an
.B ignoreip
entry written without a duration, and
.B fail2zig ignore add
without
.BR \-\-for .
Unset or 0 means permanent, the default. A jail value overrides
.BR [defaults] ,
and 0 in a jail restores permanent under a
.B [defaults]
value. A duration written on the entry, or
.BR \-\-for ,
wins. A configured entry's period follows TIMED ENTRIES, so changing
.B ignoretime
starts a fresh period for every entry that takes it. A runtime entry added without
.B \-\-for
expires
.B ignoretime
after the add and is then deleted. A value above 3162240000 seconds (100 \(mu 366 days) is
refused as an invalid duration. A jail with
.B rule_files
whose resolved value is not 0 is refused with
.BI "jails." name ".ignoretime: a default ignore duration is not supported with rule_files" .
A change is applied live by
.BR "fail2zig reload" .
.TP
.B source
String: auto, file, journald, or internal. Default: auto. Inherited by jails.
Auto uses existing log files, then supported journald input; recidive can use
internal confirmed-ban events. Explicit journald supports sshd; internal
supports recidive. Journald and internal sources require no logpath.
.TP
.B journal_executables
String array of absolute executable paths accepted as journal origins for an
sshd jail. When omitted for the built-in sshd filter without custom rule_files,
the daemon discovers available trusted SSH executables from a fixed list:
/usr/sbin/sshd, /usr/bin/sshd, and sshd-session/sshd-auth beneath
/usr/lib/openssh, /usr/lib/ssh, and /usr/libexec/openssh.
Discovery resolves and deduplicates symlinks, requires root-owned directories
and symlinks, and rejects group/other-writable directories or executables.
At least one root-owned regular executable must qualify. Missing optional paths
are skipped; unsafe existing paths cause startup refusal. PATH is not searched.
.PP
An explicit list replaces discovery completely and retains its configured order
and spelling; an explicit empty list is rejected. Custom journal rules require
an explicit nonempty list. Each explicit path must be a root-owned regular
executable without group/other write permission.
.PP
Existing explicit profiles retain the 0.4.0 journal selectors for sshd and
sshd-session. Automatic profiles that discover sshd-auth also select that name.
The effective paths and query are part of saved source identity. Changed SSH
layouts or switching profile modes may cause RetryGenerationMismatch on restart;
live source-generation migration is not supported. Restore the previous source
configuration and executable layout to recover; do not delete the state database.
Adding or enabling a jail is not such a change: the other jails keep their saved
identity and the daemon starts on the existing state.
.TP
.B backend
Deprecated source alias for fail2ban compatibility (systemd means journald).
Do not specify both source and backend in the same section. It does not select
a firewall backend; use global.firewall.
.TP
.B bantime_increment_enabled
Boolean. Enable escalating ban durations for repeat offenders. Default:
.IR false .
.TP
.B bantime_increment_multiplier
Positive integer or decimal. Base multiplier in the escalation formula. Default: 1.
.TP
.B bantime_increment_factor
Nonnegative integer or decimal. Growth rate in the escalation formula. Default: 1.
.TP
.B bantime_increment_formula
String.
.B linear
or
.BR exponential .
.I linear:
.IR "bantime × multiplier × (1 + factor × n)" .
.I exponential:
.IR "bantime × multiplier × factor^n" .
Default:
.IR linear .
.TP
.B bantime_increment_max_bantime
Integer seconds or a quoted duration. Cap on escalated ban durations.
Minimum: 1. Default: 604800 (7 days).
.TP
.B bantime_increment_jitter
Integer seconds or a quoted duration. Maximum escalation jitter; zero disables
jitter. Must not exceed bantime_increment_max_bantime. Default: 0.
.TP
.B bantime_increment_decay
Integer seconds or a quoted duration. When a subject's last ban ended more than this long
ago, its escalation count resets to zero and its next ban gets the base
.BR bantime .
The time is measured from the end of the ban, not its start, so a count never decays
during a live ban, and a permanent ban never ends. The reset is complete, never a step
down. 0 disables decay. A change is applied live by
.B fail2zig reload
and is not part of the detection generation. Default: 2592000 (30 days).
.
.SH DURATION SYNTAX
.BR bantime ,
.BR findtime ,
.BR bantime_increment_max_bantime ,
.BR bantime_increment_jitter ,
.B bantime_increment_decay
and
.B ignoretime
accept integer seconds or quoted duration strings, in defaults and jail overrides. The
duration part of a
.B banip
or
.B ignoreip
entry uses the same terms inside the entry's string, written as one word such as
.BR 1h30m ;
a third word makes the entry invalid.
Each term is a nonnegative decimal integer immediately followed by
.BR s , m , mm , min , h , d , w , mo " or " y .
The units m, mm and min all mean minutes; mo means 2,629,800 seconds and y means
31,557,600 seconds. Months and years are fixed intervals, not calendar arithmetic.
.PP
Terms may be concatenated or separated by whitespace, for example "1h30m" or
"1h 30m". Fractional terms, signs, arithmetic expressions, unknown units and
overflow are rejected. A bare value such as 24h is not valid TOML.
Converted values retain the existing field limits and zero rules; "0s" is
valid for jitter but not finite bantime, findtime or the escalation cap.
0 disables
.B bantime_increment_decay
and makes
.B ignoretime
permanent. An entry's duration must be finite, at least 1s and at most
3162240000 seconds.
Only bantime accepts "permanent". CLI duration and timeout arguments retain
their existing integer syntax, except
.B fail2zig ignore add \-\-for
which takes this syntax.
.
.SH TIMED ENTRIES
A
.B banip
or
.B ignoreip
entry written with a duration, and an
.B ignoreip
entry that takes
.BR ignoretime ,
is timed. Its period starts when the daemon first applies the entry, at start or at a
reload, and the start and the deadline are stored in the state file. The period runs out
once: a reload or a restart does not restart it. Changing the entry's duration, or the
.B ignoretime
an entry takes, starts a fresh period, as does removing the entry, reloading, and adding
it back. When the period runs out the entry stays in the file and does nothing, and
.B fail2zig list
or
.B fail2zig ignore list
shows it as
.B expired
until it is removed from the file. A reload that changes only a duration is applied live.
.
.SH JAIL SECTIONS
.PP
The jail-only key
.B history_max_matches
is an optional integer from 0 to 1024. Omission inherits the global value;
.B [defaults]
does not define it. Its effective value is capped by the global total.
For example, global 10 and jail 2 retain at most two matches for that jail and
at most ten for the address across all jails. Removing a jail removes its override;
its retained history then uses the global value. Accepted changes apply on reload.
Each
.BI [jails. name ]
section defines one jail. The name must be ASCII alphanumeric, hyphens, or
underscores.
.TP
.B enabled
Boolean. When false, the jail is parsed but not started. Default:
.IR true .
On restart, disabling a previously used jail retires its retry and source state
and releases its firewall ownership. Confirmed history, saved runtime ignore
entries and configured ignore deadlines remain. Re-enabling starts fresh retry
and source state; it does not restore the retired bans or retry window.
.TP
.B filter
String. Built-in filter name, or the bounded detection label for a jail with
.BR rule_files .
Built-in names accept hyphenated and underscore forms interchangeably. Without
.BR rule_files ,
an enabled jail must name a supported built-in filter; empty or unknown names
fail validation.
.TP
.B rule_files
Optional string array of one to eight unique absolute paths to bounded native
JSON rules. Each file is limited to 4096 bytes, must be a regular file owned by
root or the daemon user, must not be group/other writable, and is opened without
following a final symlink. Native ingestion must be enabled; the jail source must
resolve to file or journald. Custom journald rules also require an explicit
nonempty
.BR journal_executables
list. Rule paths and contents are admitted at startup. Changes are restart-only
and may be refused when durable retry or consumer state is bound to the prior
generation; restore the prior rule and source configuration to recover rather
than deleting state. Use
.B fail2zig rule-test \-\-rule\-file
to evaluate one rule without starting the daemon.
.TP
.B logpath
String array. Absolute paths to log files. Missing files are warned about but
are not fatal — they may appear after the daemon starts.
.TP
.B source
Overrides defaults.source. Journald and internal jails do not need logpath.
.TP
.B maxretry
Optional integer (1–128). Overrides
.B defaults.maxretry
for this jail.
.TP
.B findtime
Optional integer seconds or quoted duration. Overrides
.B defaults.findtime
for this jail.
.TP
.B bantime
Optional integer seconds, quoted duration or "permanent". Overrides
.B defaults.bantime
for this jail.
.TP
.B banaction
Deprecated optional compatibility string. Overrides
.B defaults.banaction
for this jail. Prefer
.B enforce
to express jail intent.
.TP
.B enforce
Optional Boolean. Overrides
.B defaults.enforce
for this jail.
.TP
.B ignoreip
Optional string array. Replaces (does not merge with)
.B defaults.ignoreip
for this jail. An edit applies from the next event after
.B fail2zig reload
or a restart, and never alters an existing ban.
.TP
.B banip
Optional string array. Replaces (does not merge with)
.B defaults.banip
for this jail.
.TP
.BR ignoreself ", " ignoretime
Optional. Override the
.B [defaults]
values for this jail.
.TP
.B on_truncation
String:
.B \&"refuse"
or
.BR \&"restart" .
Default when absent:
.BR refuse .
Accepted only in a
.BI [jails. name ]
section whose source resolves to file, where it applies to every
.B logpath
of the jail. It is rejected in
.BR [defaults] ,
so accepting loss is chosen for each source and never inherited, and in a jail
whose source is journald or internal. The diagnostics are
.BR InvalidTruncationPolicy ,
.B TruncationPolicyNotFileSource
and
.BR TruncationPolicyNotAllowedInDefaults .
.IP
Three events are covered: a file truncated in place below its committed offset,
a rotated-out file that was removed while it still held unread bytes, and a log
replaced by a different file when its previous file exists nowhere in its
directory (for example deleted and recreated by its writer). With
.BR refuse ,
the source is paused until
.B fail2zig repair-source
acknowledges the loss; the daemon still starts and the other jails enforce. A
source whose path is absent stays paused and
.B repair-source
does not acknowledge it. With
.BR restart ,
the source resumes without pausing: at offset 0 of the same file after a
truncation, at the current file's committed offset after a removed rotated
file, or at offset 0 of the replacement after a replaced log. A rotated file that
still exists in the directory is followed, never skipped. Each resumption records a
durable continuity-loss event, shown by
.B fail2zig status
and the journal; the newest 256 events per source are kept. While the path is
absent, a
.B restart
source reports health
.B missing
and retries on the transient schedule instead of pausing, then resumes when a file
appears at the path.
.IP
Records appended after the committed offset and removed by the truncation, or left
in a replaced file that is gone, were never read. The policy records that loss; it
does not recover those records.
.IP
The key is not part of the detection generation, so changing it never causes
RetryGenerationMismatch. A change is restart-only:
.B fail2zig reload
reports
.B restart_required
with the reason
\fBjails.\fIname\fB.on_truncation\fR.
.B fail2zig \-\-validate-config
prints
.BI on_truncation= value
on the summary line of each file jail.
.TP
.B bantime_increment_enabled , bantime_increment_multiplier , bantime_increment_factor , bantime_increment_formula , bantime_increment_max_bantime , bantime_increment_jitter , bantime_increment_decay
Per-jail values take precedence over
.B [defaults]
values; jails without an explicit setting inherit from defaults.
.B bantime_increment_multiplier
and
.B bantime_increment_factor
accept either integer or fractional values (e.g.
.B factor = 1.5
for a softer escalation curve).
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Minimal configuration
.nf
[global]
memory_ceiling_mb = 64

[defaults]
bantime   = 600
findtime  = 600
maxretry  = 5
enforce  = true
ignoreip  = ["127.0.0.1/8", "::1"]

[jails.sshd]
enabled = true
filter  = "sshd"
source  = "journald"
journal_executables = ["/usr/sbin/sshd", "/usr/lib/openssh/sshd-session"]
.fi
.PP
.SS SSH with exponential escalation
.nf
[defaults]
bantime                       = 600
findtime                      = 600
maxretry                      = 5
enforce                        = true
ignoreip                      = ["127.0.0.1/8", "::1"]
bantime_increment_enabled     = true
bantime_increment_formula     = "exponential"
bantime_increment_multiplier  = 1
bantime_increment_factor      = 2
bantime_increment_max_bantime = 1209600

[jails.sshd]
enabled  = true
filter   = "sshd"
source   = "journald"
journal_executables = ["/usr/sbin/sshd", "/usr/lib/openssh/sshd-session"]
maxretry = 3
bantime  = 3600
.fi
.
.SS Configuration bans and timed exclusions
.nf
[defaults]
ignoretime = "1d"

[jails.sshd]
enabled  = true
filter   = "sshd"
source   = "journald"
banip    = ["203.0.113.4", "198.51.100.0/24 7d"]
ignoreip = ["192.0.2.10", "192.0.2.20 2h"]
.fi
.PP
203.0.113.4 is banned for as long as it is listed. 198.51.100.0/24 is banned for seven
days from the first start or reload that applies it, then shows as
.B expired
in
.BR "fail2zig list" .
192.0.2.10 takes
.B ignoretime
and is excluded for one day; 192.0.2.20 is excluded for two hours. Every host address is
also excluded, because
.B ignoreself
defaults to true.
.
.SS PortSentry v2.0.7 history file (v0.4.3)
This v0.4.3 integration targets the upstream PortSentry v2.0.7 history
file at
.IR /var/log/portsentry.log .
It does not support the legacy PortSentry 1.2 format, including a same-named
Fail2ban filter; migration refuses that incompatible mapping. The Debian 13
standard repository package is legacy 1.2 and is distinct from the pinned
upstream 2.0.7 target.
.PP
Run PortSentry with
.B \-\-connect
for TCP connect detection, or with
.B \-\-stealth \-m pcap \-i
.I interface
for TCP SYN, FIN, NULL and XMAS scans and UDP.
.B \-\-stealth
runs PortSentry as root with pcap on the interface that owns the protected
address. Retain one TCP_PORTS list containing ports verified unused on the
protected host, and one UDP_PORTS list of verified unused ports when UDP
detection is wanted. Set HISTORY_FILE=/var/log/portsentry.log, RESOLVE_HOST=0,
SCAN_TRIGGER=0, BLOCK_TCP=0 and BLOCK_UDP=0. To observe TCP only, remove or
comment every UDP_PORTS directive; do not set UDP_PORTS="", because PortSentry
rejects an empty list. BLOCK_UDP=0 disables PortSentry's UDP response but does
not stop UDP observations when UDP ports remain configured. These settings leave
fail2zig as the only ban owner.
.PP
.nf
[jails.portsentry]
enabled    = true
filter     = "portsentry"
source     = "file"
timestamp  = "iso8601"
logpath    = ["/var/log/portsentry.log"]
maxretry   = 1
enforce = true
on_truncation = "restart"
.fi
.PP
PortSentry 2.0.7 empties its history file at every process start, not only at
reboot. Without
.BR on_truncation ,
the default
.B refuse
pauses the source after each PortSentry restart until
.B fail2zig repair-source
acknowledges it. With
.BR restart ,
the jail resumes at offset 0 and records a continuity-loss event; records that
PortSentry appended after the committed offset and that the emptying removed were
never read.
.PP
Accepted events protect the source host across all protocols and ports. The
live qualification on Debian 13 covered IPv4 and IPv6 TCP-connect events
and, in stealth mode, TCP SYN, FIN, NULL and XMAS scans and UDP, each through
ban, blocking while banned and expiry. Legacy PortSentry 1.2 and other 2.x
versions are unqualified, and stdout, syslog and journal envelopes are outside
this integration.
.PP
Inspect a history file without starting the daemon:
.PP
.nf
fail2zig rule-test --file /var/log/portsentry.log --service portsentry --output table
.fi
.
